Here's the config format for MCP servers in Claude Code.

## Where it lives

**Not `settings.json`** — that file is never read for MCP config, even though most other extension mechanisms do live there.

| Scope | File | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Project | `.mcp.json` at project root | Meant to be committed to version control, shared with the team |
| Local (default) | `~/.claude.json`, scoped to that project's path | Loads only in that project; the usual reason a server "vanishes" after `cd` |
| User | `~/.claude.json` | Available across all your projects |

`~/.claude.json` is one character off from `~/.claude/settings.json` and a completely different file — easy to mix up.

## JSON schema per transport

**stdio** (local child process):
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-server": {
      "command": "node",
      "args": ["./server.js"],
      "env": { "API_KEY": "${MY_API_KEY}" }
    }
  }
}
```

**HTTP** (remote, supports OAuth):
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-server": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://example.com/mcp",
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer ${TOKEN}" }
    }
  }
}
```

**SSE** (deprecated, prefer HTTP):
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "my-server": {
      "type": "sse",
      "url": "https://example.com/sse"
    }
  }
}
```

**WebSocket** — only reachable by writing `"type": "ws"` by hand or via `claude mcp add-json`; there's no `--transport ws` CLI flag. A `url` entry with no `type` is a config error, not an inferred default.

## Building it via CLI instead of hand-editing

```
claude mcp add [options] <name> -- <command> [args...]
```
- `-s`/`--scope` → `local` | `project` | `user` (chooses which file gets the entry)
- `-t`/`--transport` → `http` | `sse` | `stdio` (omit for local stdio)
- `-e`/`--env KEY=value` for env vars (repeatable; don't put the server name directly after `--env` — it'll be misread as another KEY=value pair)
- `-H`/`--header "Name: value"` for static headers on remote servers

Or hand a full JSON object: `claude mcp add-json <name> '<json>'` (respects `--scope`).

`${VAR}` / `${VAR:-default}` expansion works in `command`, `args`, `env`, `url`, and `headers` — useful for keeping secrets out of the committed `.mcp.json`: commit the placeholder, let the loader expand it from your actual environment. Note an unset var with no default doesn't fail the load — it silently passes the literal `${VAR}` through with only a warning in `claude mcp list`.

Claude Code stores MCP server configuration in `.mcp.json` (project scope) or in user/global config, and also supports adding servers via `claude mcp add`. Here's the JSON structure:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "server-name": {
      "command": "npx",
      "args": ["-y", "@some-org/mcp-server-package"],
      "env": {
        "API_KEY": "your-key-here"
      }
    },
    "another-server-http": {
      "type": "http",
      "url": "https://example.com/mcp",
      "headers": {
        "Authorization": "Bearer your-token"
      }
    },
    "another-server-sse": {
      "type": "sse",
      "url": "https://example.com/sse"
    }
  }
}
```

Key points:

- **`command`/`args`/`env`** — for local stdio servers (spawns a subprocess speaking MCP over stdio).
- **`type: "http"`** or **`type: "sse"`** with **`url`** — for remote servers, optionally with **`headers`** for auth.
- **Scopes**: 
  - Project-level: `.mcp.json` at the repo root (checked into git, shared with team).
  - User-level (global, all projects): stored in Claude Code's user config.
  - Local (per-project, not shared): stored in local settings.
- You can manage these without hand-editing JSON via the CLI:
  ```
  claude mcp add <name> -- <command> [args...]
  claude mcp add-json <name> '<json-config>'
  claude mcp add --transport http <name> <url>
  claude mcp list
  claude mcp remove <name>
  ```
- Inside an interactive session, `/mcp` shows configured servers and their connection/auth status.

Since this environment shows several MCP servers already connected (Hugging Face, Postman, Microsoft Learn, etc.) plus others needing auth (Gmail, Google Drive, etc.), your existing config likely already has a `mcpServers` block set up — want me to look for and show you the actual config file on this machine, or are you setting up a new server?

`CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS` is an environment variable that sets the maximum number of output tokens Claude Code requests from the model per API call (it overrides the default `max_tokens` sent with each request). Raising it lets Claude produce longer single responses (useful for big code generations); it doesn't affect input/context size.

How to set it:
- Shell: `export CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192` (bash) or `$env:CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=8192` (PowerShell)
- Persistently via `settings.json`'s `env` block:
```json
{
  "env": {
    "CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS": "8192"
  }
}
```

Default and valid range depend on the model in use (each Claude model has its own max-output ceiling — see the Claude API docs for current per-model limits); setting this env var above a model's actual ceiling won't exceed what the model supports.

Note: this answer is general knowledge, not sourced from the extension-engineering skill I loaded (it covers authoring hooks/skills/subagents/etc., not this kind of operating/config variable). The authoritative source is the Claude Code settings documentation page: `https://code.claude.com/docs/en/settings`.

`CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS` is an environment variable that caps the maximum tokens Claude Code will request/generate in a single model response (i.e., it's passed as `max_tokens` to the API call). It's useful if you're hitting truncated responses on long generations, or conversely want to cap spend/latency by limiting response length.

Ways to set it when working in VS Code:

1. **Claude Code settings.json** (recommended, works regardless of terminal) — add an `env` block to `.claude/settings.json` (project) or the user-level settings:
```json
{
  "env": {
    "CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS": "8192"
  }
}
```
2. **VS Code integrated terminal only** — set it in `settings.json` under `terminal.integrated.env.windows`:
```json
"terminal.integrated.env.windows": {
  "CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS": "8192"
}
```
3. **Shell profile** (PowerShell `$PROFILE`) — `$env:CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS = "8192"`, so it's inherited by any terminal VS Code opens.

